 C11=7, C12=-19, C13=-11, C21=1, C22=-1, C23=-1, C31=-3, C32=11, C33=7 [1½]\\nadj(A) = [[7,1,-3],[-19,-1,11],[-11,-1,7]] [½]\\nA⁻¹ = (1/4)adj(A) [½]\\nX = A⁻¹B: x = (1/4)(49-5-36) = 8/4 = 2, y = (1/4)(-133-(-5)+132) = 4/4 = 1, z = (1/4)(-77-(-5)+84) = 12/4 = 3 [1½]\\nAnswer: x = 2, y = 1, z = 3\\n\\nQ34 (5 marks):\\ny = (tan⁻¹x)²\\ny₁ = 2tan⁻¹x · 1/(1+x²) [1]\\n(1+x²)y₁ = 2tan⁻¹x ... (*) [1]\\nDifferentiate (*): (1+x²)y₂ + 2xy₁ = 2/(1+x²) [1½]\\nMultiply by (1+x²): (1+x²)²y₂ + 2x(1+x²)y₁ = 2 [1]\\nHence proved [½]\\n\\nQ35 (5 marks):\\n(x+1)(x+2) = x²+3x+2\\nLong division: (x²+x+1)/(x²+3x+2) = 1 + (-2x-1)/(x²+3x+2) [1]\\nPartial fractions: (-2x-1)/((x+1)(x+2)) = A/(x+1) + B/(x+2) [½]\\nx=-1: 1 = A → A = 1; x=-2: 3 = -B → B = -3 [1½]\\n∫ [1 + 1/(x+1) - 3/(x+2)] dx [1]\\n= x + ln|x+1| - 3ln|x+2| + C [1]\\n\\nQ36 (4 marks):\\n(i) Reflexive: Every a ∈ A divides itself, so (a,a) ∈ R ✓ [½]. Transitive: If a|b and b|c then a|c, so (a,b) ∈ R and (b,c) ∈ R ⟹ (a,c) ∈ R ✓ [1½].\\n(ii) R is NOT symmetric. Counter-example: (1,2) ∈ R (1 divides 2) but (2,1) ∉ R (2 does not divide 1) [1].\\n(iii) R is NOT an equivalence relation because it is not symmetric [1].\\n\\nQ37 (4 marks):\\n(i) MR(x) = dR/dx = 26x + 26 [2]\\n(ii) MR(7) = 26(7) + 26 = 182 + 26 = 208 [1]\\n(iii) Since MR(7) = 208 > 0, revenue is increasing at x = 7 [1]\\n\\nQ38 (4 marks):\\n(i) s(t) = ∫(6t²-4t)dt = 2t³ - 2t² + C; s(0)=0 ⟹ C=0. So s(t) = 2t³ - 2t² [2]\\n(ii) s(3) = 2(27) - 2(9) = 54 - 18 = 36 m [1]\\n(iii) v(t) = 0: 6t²-4t = 0 → 2t(3t-2) = 0 → t = 2/3 s (for t > 0) [1]\\n\\nIMPORTANT: Write both files completely. Do not truncate or abbreviate any content. Use Overwrite=false for both files.\",\n    \"inherit\": true,\n    \"model\": \"MODEL_PLACEHOLDER_M301\",\n    \"modelTier\": \"MODEL_TIER_FLASH\"\n  },\n  \"result\": {\n    \"conversationId\": \"fc6abd1e-d22c-4e86-9e86-8f33197c6466\",\n    \"logAbsoluteUri\": \"file:///C:/Users/rajan/.gemini/antigravity/brain/fc6abd1e-d22c-4e86-9e86-8f33197c6466/.system_