{"step_index":186,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T16:59:29Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":false,"Summary":"Sync text files with exact official DAV header template and asterisks.","UserFacing":false},"CodeContent":"\"\"\"\nSync text files with exact DAV Institutions, Jharkhand Zone-F header format.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport os\n\nBASE_DIR = r\"C:\\Users\\rajan\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\math_exam_2026\"\n\ndef update_txt_files():\n    # Set A Question Paper\n    with open(os.path.join(BASE_DIR, \"Set_A_Question_Paper.txt\"), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        f.write(\"\"\"+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     DAV INSTITUTIONS, JHARKHAND ZONE-F                       |\n|                       Half Yearly Examination, 2026-27                       |\n|                                                                              |\n| Class: XII                                              FULL MARKS: 80       |\n| Subject: Mathematics (Set A)                            TIME ALLOWED: 3 HOURS|\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n                                  ************\n\nGeneral Instructions:\n1. This question paper contains five sections — A, B, C, D and E. All questions are compulsory.\n2. Section A consists of 20 questions of 1 mark each (Q1–Q20).\n3. Question numbers 19 and 20 are Assertion–Reason based questions. Two statements are given, marked Assertion (A) and Reason (R). Select the correct option from the four given below each question.\n4. Section B consists of 5 questions of 2 marks each (Q21–Q25).\n5. Section C consists of 6 questions of 3 marks each (Q26–Q31).\n6. Section D consists of 4 questions of 5 marks each (Q32–Q35).\n7. Section E consists of 3 case-based/source-based questions of 4 marks each (Q36–Q38), with sub-parts of 2, 1 and 1 marks.\n8. There is no overall choice. All questions are compulsory as set.\n9. Use of calculators is not permitted.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n                                  SECTION A\n(Question numbers 1 to 18 carry 1 mark each. Questions 19 and 20 are Assertion–Reason based, 1 mark each.)\n\n1. Let A = {1, 2, 3} and R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,2)} be a relation on A. Then R is:\n(a) An equivalence relation\n(b) Reflexive and transitive only\n(c) Reflexive and symmetric only\n(d) Symmetric and transitive only\n\n2. The function f : ℝ → ℝ defined by f(x) = x² is:\n(a) One-one and onto\n(b) One-one but not onto\n(c) Onto but not one-one\n(d) Neither one-one nor onto\n\n3. The principal value of sin⁻¹(−1/2) is:\n(a) π/6            (b) −π/6           (c) 5π/6           (d) −5π/6\n\n4. The domain of cos⁻¹(2x − 1) is:\n(a) [0, 1]         (b) [−1, 1]        (c) [−1, 0]        (d) [0, 2]\n\n5. The range of the principal value branch of tan⁻¹x is:\n(a) [−π/2, π/2]    (b) (−π/2, π/2)    (c) [0, π]         (d) (0, π)\n\n6. The value of x for which the matrix A = [[0, 1, -2], [-1, 0, x], [2, -3, 0]] is skew-symmetric, is:\n(a) 3              (b) −3             (c) 2              (d) −2\n\n7. For any square matrix A, the matrix (A + Aᵀ) is always:\n(a) Skew-symmetric                     (b) Symmetric\n(c) A diagonal matrix                  (d) A null matrix\n\n8. If A and B are symmetric matrices of same order, then (AB − BA) is a:\n(a) Symmetric matrix                   (b) Skew-symmetric matrix\n(c) Null matrix                        (d) Identity matrix\n\n9. If A is a 3 × 3 matrix such that |A| = 5, then the value of |3A| is:\n(a) 15             (b) 45             (c) 135            (d) 405\n\n10. If A is a square matrix of order 3 with |A| = 4, then |adj A| equals:\n(a) 4              (b) 8              (c) 16             (d) 64\n\n11. If y = e²ˣ, then dy/dx equals:\n(a) e²ˣ            (b) 2e²ˣ           (c) 2x e²ˣ         (d) eˣ\n\n12. The rate of change of the area of a circle with respect to its radius r, at r = 5 cm, is:\n(a) 5π cm²/cm      (b) 10π cm²/cm     (c) 25π cm²/cm     (d) 2π cm²/cm\n\n13. ∫ sec²x dx equals:\n(a) tan x + C      (b) −cot x + C     (c) sec x tan x + C (d) cot x + C\n\n14. ∫ 1/(1 + x²) dx equals:\n(a) tan⁻¹x + C     (b) sin⁻¹x + C     (c) cot⁻¹x + C     (d) sec⁻¹x + C\n\n15. ∫ 1/√(1 − x²) dx equals:\n(a) sin⁻¹x + C     (b) cos⁻¹x + C     (c) tan⁻¹x + C     (d) sec⁻¹x + C\n\n16. ∫ e³ˣ dx equals:\n(a) e³ˣ + C        (b) 3e³ˣ + C       (c) (1/3)e³ˣ + C   (d) eˣ + C\n\n17. ∫ cos(2x) dx equals:\n(a) sin(2x) + C    (b) (1/2)sin(2x) + C (c) −2sin(2x) + C (d) −(1/2)sin(2x) + C\n\n18. ∫ (1/x) dx (for x > 0) equals:\n(a) 1/x² + C       (b) −1/x² + C      (c) ln(x) + C      (d) x + C\n\n19. The graph of a function y = f(x) is shown below:\n[Figure 19: Graph of f(x) = |x - 2|]\nAssertion (A): The function f(x) = |x − 2| shown in the graph is continuous at x = 2.\nReason (R): The function f(x) = |x − 2| is differentiable at x = 2.\n(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true, but R is false.\n(d) A is false, but R is true.\n\n20. The graph of a function y = f(x) is shown below:\n[Figure 20: Graph of f(x) = -(x - 3)² + 4]\nAssertion (A): The point (3, 4) marked on the graph of f(x) = −(x − 3)² + 4 is a point of local maximum.\nReason (R): At a point of local maximum, f′(x) = 0 and f″(x) < 0.\n(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true, but R is false.\n(d) A is false, but R is true.\n\n                                  SECTION B\n(Question numbers 21 to 25 carry 2 marks each.)\n\n21. Check whether the relation R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,2), (2,3)} on the set A = {1, 2, 3} is reflexive, symmetric and transitive. Justify your answer.\n\n22. Find the principal value of: tan⁻¹(√3) − sec⁻¹(−2).\n\n23. If f(x) = kx + 1 for x ≤ 5 and f(x) = 3x − 5 for x > 5 is continuous at x = 5, find the value of k.\n\n24. Differentiate y = sin(x² + 1) with respect to x.\n\n25. Evaluate: ∫ x e^(x²) dx.\n\n                                  SECTION C\n(Question numbers 26 to 31 carry 3 marks each.)\n\n26. Find the value of:\n(i) sin⁻¹(sin(2π/3))\n(ii) cos⁻¹(cos(7π/6))\n(iii) tan⁻¹(tan(3π/4))\n\n27. If A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 3]], find (2A − 3B) and verify that (A + B)ᵀ = Aᵀ + Bᵀ.\n\n28. Using minors and cofactors, evaluate the determinant:\n    |A| = | 1   2   3 |\n          | 0   1   4 |\n          | 5   6   0 |\n\n29. If y = xˣ (x > 0), find dy/dx.\n\n30. Find the intervals in which the function f(x) = 2x³ − 15x² + 36x + 1 is (i) strictly increasing (ii) strictly decreasing.\n\n31. Evaluate ∫ x sin x dx using integration by parts.\n\n                                  SECTION D\n(Question numbers 32 to 35 carry 5 marks each.)\n\n32. For the system of linear equations:\n    x + y + z = 6\n    y + 3z = 11\n    x − 2y + z = 0\nwrite the coefficient matrix A = [[1, 1, 1], [0, 1, 3], [1, -2, 1]]. Evaluate the determinant |A|, find adj(A), determine A⁻¹ = adj(A)/|A|, and hence solve the system of equations.\n\n33. If y = (sin⁻¹x)², prove that (1 − x²) d²y/dx² − x dy/dx − 2 = 0.\n\n34. An open topped box is to be constructed by removing equal squares of side x from each corner of a 24 cm × 9 cm rectangular sheet of tin and folding up the sides. Find the value of x for which the volume of the box is maximum, and determine this maximum volume.\n\n35. Evaluate: ∫ (x² + 1)/(x² − 5x + 6) dx.\n\n                                  SECTION E\n(Question numbers 36 to 38 are case-based questions of 4 marks each, with sub-parts of 2, 1 and 1 marks.)\n\n36. Case Study 1 — Relations and Functions\nTwo friends, Rohan and Simran, are studying equivalence relations on sets. Consider the set A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} and the relation R on A defined by R = {(a, b) : |a − b| is even}.\n[Figure 36: Partition of Set A into Equivalence Classes]\nBased on the above information, answer the following:\n(i) Show that R is an equivalence relation on A.                             (2 marks)\n(ii) Write the equivalence class of the element 2.                          (1 mark)\n(iii) Is (1, 5) ∈ R? Justify your answer.                                  (1 mark)\n\n37. Case Study 2 — Matrices\nA school management committee decides to award prizes to students for three core values: Discipline (x), Cleanliness (y), and Regularity (z). Two schools P and Q decide to award their students as per the allocation scheme shown in Figure 37:\n[Figure 37: Prize Allocation Scheme for Schools P & Q]\nBased on the above information, answer the following:\n(i) Represent the given problem in the form of a matrix equation AX = B.     (2 marks)\n(ii) If the prize money for Discipline is ₹ 500 and Regularity is ₹ 600, calculate the prize money for Cleanliness. (1 mark)\n(iii) State one moral/social benefit of recognizing Cleanliness among students. (1 mark)\n\n38. Case Study 3 — Application of Derivatives\nAn automobile manufacturing company models its commercial performance. The total revenue received (in thousands of ₹) from the sale of x units of cars is given by R(x) = 3x² + 36x + 5, while the total cost of production is given by C(x) = 0.005x³ − 0.02x² + 30x + 5000.\n[Figure 38: Revenue Analysis Curve]\nBased on the above information, answer the following:\n(i) Find the marginal revenue function, MR(x) = dR/dx, and evaluate it at x = 10 cars. (2 marks)\n(ii) Find the marginal cost when x = 2 cars.                               (1 mark)\n(iii) Write the profit function P(x) in terms of R(x) and C(x).             (1 mark)\n\n— End of Question Paper —\n\"\"\")\n\n    # Set B Question Paper\n    with open(os.path.join(BASE_DIR, \"Set_B_Question_Paper.txt\"), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        f.write(\"\"\"+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     DAV INSTITUTIONS, JHARKHAND ZONE-F                       |\n|                       Half Yearly Examination, 2026-27                       |\n|                                                                              |\n| Class: XII                                              FULL MARKS: 80       |\n| Subject: Mathematics (Set B)                            TIME ALLOWED: 3 HOURS|\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n                                  ************\n\nGeneral Instructions:\n1. This question paper contains five sections — A, B, C, D and E. All questions are compulsory.\n2. Section A consists of 20 questions of 1 mark each (Q1–Q20).\n3. Question numbers 19 and 20 are Assertion–Reason based questions. Two statements are given, marked Assertion (A) and Reason (R). Select the correct option from the four given below each question.\n4. Section B consists of 5 questions of 2 marks each (Q21–Q25).\n5. Section C consists of 6 questions of 3 marks each (Q26–Q31).\n6. Section D consists of 4 questions of 5 marks each (Q32–Q35).\n7. Section E consists of 3 case-based/source-based questions of 4 marks each (Q36–Q38), with sub-parts of 2, 1 and 1 marks.\n8. There is no overall choice. All questions are compulsory as set.\n9. Use of calculators is not permitted.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n                                  SECTION A\n(Question numbers 1 to 18 carry 1 mark each. Questions 19 and 20 are Assertion–Reason based, 1 mark each.)\n\n1. Let R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,3), (3,1)} be a relation on A = {1, 2, 3}. Then R is:\n(a) Reflexive only\n(b) Symmetric only\n(c) An equivalence relation\n(d) Reflexive and symmetric only\n\n2. The number of all one-one functions from the set {1, 2, 3} to itself is:\n(a) 3              (b) 6              (c) 9              (d) 27\n\n3. The principal value of cos⁻¹(−1/√2) is:\n(a) π/4            (b) 3π/4           (c) −π/4           (d) 5π/4\n\n4. The domain of sin⁻¹(3x − 1) is:\n(a) [0, 2/3]       (b) [−1, 1]        (c) [0, 1]         (d) [−2/3, 2/3]\n\n5. The principal value of tan⁻¹(−1) is:\n(a) π/4            (b) −π/4           (c) 3π/4           (d) −3π/4\n\n6. If the matrix A = [[0, a, -3], [2, 0, -1], [b, 1, 0]] is skew-symmetric, then the value of (a + b) is:\n(a) 1              (b) −1             (c) 5              (d) −5\n\n7. A matrix A = [aᵢⱼ]₃ₓ₃ is a diagonal matrix if:\n(a) aᵢⱼ = 0 for all i, j               (b) aᵢⱼ = 0 for i ≠ j\n(c) aᵢⱼ = 0 for i = j                  (d) aᵢⱼ ≠ 0 for all i, j\n\n8. If A and B are square matrices of the same order, then (A + B)ᵀ equals:\n(a) Aᵀ + Bᵀ        (b) Aᵀ − Bᵀ        (c) AᵀBᵀ           (d) BᵀAᵀ\n\n9. If A is a 3 × 3 matrix with |A| = −2, then |3A| equals:\n(a) −6             (b) −54            (c) 54             (d) −18\n\n10. The area of the triangle with vertices (1, 0), (6, 0) and (4, 3), found using determinants, is:\n(a) 15 sq units    (b) 15/2 sq units  (c) 7 sq units     (d) 12 sq units\n\n11. If y = sin(3x), then dy/dx is:\n(a) cos(3x)        (b) 3cos(3x)       (c) −3cos(3x)      (d) 3sin(3x)\n\n12. The rate of change of the volume V = (4/3)πr³ of a sphere with respect to its radius r is:\n(a) 4πr²           (b) 2πr            (c) (4/3)πr²       (d) 4πr\n\n13. ∫ cos x dx equals:\n(a) sin x + C      (b) −sin x + C     (c) cos x + C      (d) −cos x + C\n\n14. ∫ 1/√(a² − x²) dx equals:\n(a) sin⁻¹(x/a) + C (b) (1/a)sin⁻¹(x/a) + C (c) cos⁻¹(x/a) + C (d) tan⁻¹(x/a) + C\n\n15. ∫ e⁻ˣ dx equals:\n(a) e⁻ˣ + C        (b) −e⁻ˣ + C       (c) eˣ + C         (d) −eˣ + C\n\n16. ∫ csc²x dx equals:\n(a) cot x + C      (b) −cot x + C     (c) tan x + C      (d) −csc x + C\n\n17. ∫ sec x tan x dx equals:\n(a) sec x + C      (b) tan x + C      (c) −sec x + C     (d) cot x + C\n\n18. ∫ x⁴ dx equals:\n(a) 4x³ + C        (b) x⁵/5 + C       (c) 5x⁵ + C        (d) x³/3 + C\n\n19. The graph of the greatest integer function y = [x] is shown below:\n[Figure 19: Step Discontinuities of Greatest Integer Function]\nAssertion (A): The function f(x) = [x] (greatest integer function) is discontinuous at every integer.\nReason (R): For any integer n, lim(x→n) [x] does not exist.\n(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true, but R is false.\n(d) A is false, but R is true.\n\n20. The graph of the logarithmic function y = ln(x) is shown below:\n[Figure 20: Graph of f(x) = ln(x)]\nAssertion (A): The function f(x) = log x is strictly increasing on (0, ∞).\nReason (R): f′(x) = 1/x > 0 for all x ∈ (0, ∞).\n(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true, but R is false.\n(d) A is false, but R is true.\n\n                                  SECTION B\n(Question numbers 21 to 25 carry 2 marks each.)\n\n21. Let A = {1, 2, 3} and R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,3), (3,1)} be a relation on A. Is R an equivalence relation? Justify your answer.\n\n22. Find the principal value of: cos⁻¹(−1/2) + 2sin⁻¹(1/2).\n\n23. The value of k for which f(x) = kx² (for x ≤ 2) and f(x) = 3 (for x > 2) is continuous at x = 2, is:\n\n24. Differentiate y = cos(x²) with respect to x.\n\n25. Evaluate: ∫ e^(sin x) · cos x dx.\n\n                                  SECTION C\n(Question numbers 26 to 31 carry 3 marks each.)\n\n26. Find the value of:\n(i) cos⁻¹(cos(5π/4))\n(ii) sin⁻¹(sin(−π/3))\n(iii) tan⁻¹(tan(5π/6))\n\n27. If A = [[3, 1], [-1, 2]] and B = [[1, 0], [-1, 2]], find (A + 2B) and verify that (A + B)ᵀ = Aᵀ + Bᵀ.\n\n28. Evaluate the determinant:\n    |A| = |  2  -1   3 |\n          |  1   2  -1 |\n          |  0   3   2 |\n\n29. If y = x^(sin x) (x > 0), find dy/dx.\n\n30. Find the intervals in which the function f(x) = x³ − 6x² + 9x + 15 is (i) strictly increasing (ii) strictly decreasing.\n\n31. Evaluate ∫ x cos x dx using integration by parts.\n\n                                  SECTION D\n(Question numbers 32 to 35 carry 5 marks each.)\n\n32. For the system of linear equations:\n    x − y + 2z = 7\n    3x + 4y − 5z = −5\n    2x − y + 3z = 12\nwrite the coefficient matrix A = [[1, -1, 2], [3, 4, -5], [2, -1, 3]]. Evaluate the determinant |A|, find adj(A), determine A⁻¹ = adj(A)/|A|, and hence solve the system of equations.\n\n33. If y = (tan⁻¹x)², show that (1 + x²)² d²y/dx² + 2x(1 + x²) dy/dx = 2.\n\n34. A rectangular sheet of tin 45 cm × 24 cm is to be made into a box without top, by cutting off squares of side x from each corner and folding up the flaps. Find the value of x for which the volume of the box is maximum, and determine this maximum volume.\n\n35. Evaluate: ∫ (x² + x + 1)/((x + 1)(x + 2)) dx.\n\n                                  SECTION E\n(Question numbers 36 to 38 are case-based questions of 4 marks each, with sub-parts of 2, 1 and 1 marks.)\n\n36. Case Study 1 — Relations and Functions\nA mathematics club defines a relation R on the set A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 6} as R = {(a, b) : a divides b, where a, b ∈ A}.\n[Figure 36: Divisibility Relation Graph on Set A]\nBased on the above information, answer the following:\n(i) Show that R is reflexive and transitive.                                 (2 marks)\n(ii) Is R symmetric? Justify with an example.                                (1 mark)\n(iii) Is R an equivalence relation? Give reason.                             (1 mark)\n\n37. Case Study 2 — Matrices\nTwo schools A and B decided to award prizes to their students for three exemplary values: Honesty (x), Hard Work (y), and Cooperation (z). The prize distribution is organized as shown in Figure 37:\n[Figure 37: Prize Allocation Scheme for Schools A & B]\nBased on the above information, answer the following:\n(i) Represent the given problem in the form of a matrix equation AX = B.     (2 marks)\n(ii) If the prize money for Honesty is ₹ 500 and Cooperation is ₹ 400, find the prize money for Hard Work. (1 mark)\n(iii) State one moral benefit of recognizing Hard Work in schools.           (1 mark)\n\n38. Case Study 3 — Application of Derivatives\nA manufacturing firm's total revenue (in ₹) received from selling x units of a product is given by R(x) = 13x² + 26x + 15.\n[Figure 38: Total Revenue Curve]\nBased on the above information, answer the following:\n(i) Find the marginal revenue function, MR(x) = dR/dx.                       (2 marks)\n(ii) Find the marginal revenue when x = 7 units.                             (1 mark)\n(iii) State, with reason, whether the revenue is increasing or decreasing at x = 7. (1 mark)\n\n— End of Question Paper —\n\"\"\")\n    print(\"Updated text files.\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    update_txt_files()\n","Description":"Sync text files with the official DAV header box.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\rajan\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\b78a5d36-6586-4c94-a487-70da0c9f5362\\scratch\\sync_txt.py","toolAction":"Syncing text files with official header","toolSummary":"Sync text files"}}]}
